        "bytes": 28377
      },
      "beforeText": "In this low-speed example, the relativistic-mass increase is too small to detect in the source's account.\nCharged-particle accelerators, however, can bring particles close to light speed.\n\nAn unverified source example claims that a particle moving $90$ km/s below light speed has $100$ times its rest mass.\nThis speed and factor do not agree with the preceding mass formula and must not be treated as a verified calculation.\n\nAnother unverified example claims that electrons moving $35$ to $40$ km/s below light speed have nearly $2000$ times their rest mass.\nThat example also claims that the electron's relativistic mass exceeds the proton's mass.\n\nThe source further claims that keeping such electrons on a circular path requires $2000$ times the magnetic force estimated without relativistic effects.\nThese numbers and their dependent conclusions remain unverified.\n\nThe qualitative point is that Newtonian mechanics alone cannot describe high-speed particle trajectories.\nCurved tracks of charged particles in a magnetic field provide observations for testing relativistic momentum.",
      "afterText": "In this low-speed example, the relativistic-mass increase is too small to detect in the source's account.\nCharged-particle accelerators, however, can bring particles close to light speed.\n\nIn the corrected source example, a particle moving approximately $15$ km/s below light speed has $100$ times its rest mass.\nIn the other corrected example, electrons moving $35$--$40$ m/s below light speed have nearly $2000$ times their rest mass.\n\nThat example also claims that the electron's relativistic mass exceeds the proton's mass.\nThe source further claims that keeping such electrons on a circular path requires $2000$ times the magnetic force estimated without relativistic effects.\n\nThe qualitative point is that Newtonian mechanics alone cannot describe high-speed particle trajectories.\nCurved tracks of charged particles in a magnetic field provide observations for testing relativistic momentum.",
